        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="d133-01" n="1" xml:id="d133-01"/>

                <head>133. Memorandum From the Executive Secretary of the Department of State
                    (Eliot) to the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Kissinger)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d133-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d133-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1971-05-25"
                        >May 25, 1971</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Contingency Study for Indo-Pakistan Hostilities</item>
                </list>
                <p>There is attached a draft contingency paper on the prospects for Indo-Pakistan
                    hostilities. I would appreciate your distributing this paper to members of the
                    Washington Special Action Group for consideration at its meeting on Wednesday
                    May 26.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Theodore L. Eliot, Jr.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d133-a1.xml" xpointer="d133-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d133-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d133-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–73, POL 1–1 INDIA–PAK.
                    Secret. This memorandum and, apparently, the attached study, were drafted by
                    Quainton and cleared by Schneider and Sisco.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d133-f2" n="2" xml:id="d133-f2-content">
                <p> Eliot conveyed to Kissinger, for circulation to the members of the WSAG, a
                    contingency study prepared in the Department of State assessing the policy
                    options for dealing with the escalating crisis in East Pakistan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
